- rank: 10
  name: CohnReznick
  url: https://www.cohnreznick.com
  startingPrice: Not published. CohnReznick publishes no fee or range on any page.
  bestFor: Companies that need a national firm with a real Los Angeles office attached.
  description: |-
    CohnReznick works across 30 industries from a Wilshire Boulevard office downtown. A second Los Angeles office sits in Woodland Hills.

    Its ownership changed in early 2025. CohnReznick's own release of 26 February 2025 describes a "strategic growth investment" from the Apax Funds. The release names no stake size and does not use the word majority.

    The same release splits the firm in two. CohnReznick LLP keeps the attest work under chief executive Kelly O'Callaghan. CohnReznick Advisory LLC takes what the release calls "tax, advisory, and other non-attest services" under chief executive David Kessler. That entity is not a licensed CPA firm.

    Two regulator findings sit on the record. The SEC ordered $1.9 million returned to investors on 8 June 2022 over the Longfin and Sequential audits. The PCAOB censured the firm and fined it $20,000 on 11 July 2023 for filing required disclosures roughly five months late.
  pros:
  - Published an article on 29 August 2026, the most recent dated item collected for this ranking.
  - Two City of Los Angeles offices, covering downtown and the Valley.
  - Its 2025 PCAOB inspection found deficiencies in two of four audits, which is better than Armanino's
    four of four.
  - The Apax investment is announced on the firm's own site, which Armanino's is not.
  cons:
  - Three separate regulator findings across the SEC and the PCAOB, more than any other firm ranked here.
  - Neither a founding year nor a headcount appears anywhere on the site, which is unusual at this size.
  - The attest and advisory split means the entity a client signs with may not be a licensed CPA firm.
  - Third parties often list its El Segundo office as a Los Angeles location. El Segundo is a separate
    city.
