faq:
- question: What is the best accounting firm in Los Angeles?
  answer: HCVT is the best overall choice. It is the largest Los Angeles based firm. It names family offices
    as a core client type. It works from two offices inside the city. Buyers should also read its 2018
    SEC order.
- question: Which Los Angeles accounting firm publishes its prices?
  answer: Armanino is the only one. Its startup bundle costs $18,487 a year for the Launch tier and $26,887
    a year for the Scale tier, both on a one year contract. Every other firm on this list quotes on request.
- question: Is Miller Kaplan a Los Angeles accounting firm?
  answer: No. Miller Kaplan prints the heading "Los Angeles" above an address at 3900 W. Alameda Avenue
    in Burbank. Burbank is a separate city with its own council and business tax.
- question: Which accounting firm in Los Angeles is best for startups?
  answer: Armanino, because it is the only firm that publishes an annual price a founder can budget against.
    SingerLewak is the better fit once a company is preparing for an audit committee or an IPO.
- question: Which Los Angeles firms are owned by private equity?
  answer: Three of the twelve. CohnReznick took an Apax investment in February 2025. Armanino appears
    on Further Global's portfolio page for 2024. NKSFB has been part of Focus Financial Partners since
    2018.
- question: Which accounting firm handles entertainment business management in Los Angeles?
  answer: NKSFB has the deepest practice, with separate service lines for recording artists, touring and
    athletes. Gursey Schneider is the alternative when litigation support or a business valuation is also
    needed.
- question: Do any Los Angeles accounting firms have SEC or PCAOB findings?
  answer: Yes. HCVT and CohnReznick both have SEC orders. CohnReznick also has a PCAOB penalty. Armanino
    and Rose Snyder & Jacobs have PCAOB inspection deficiencies. Each finding is named inside that firm's
    entry.
- question: How do I check a Los Angeles CPA firm myself?
  answer: Check the city on the address line rather than the heading above it. Search the firm name at
    sec.gov and pcaobus.org for orders and inspection reports. Then ask the firm who owns it today, which
    entity signs the engagement letter, and what its last peer review said.
---
## Which firm fits which buyer?

The right accounting firm in Los Angeles depends on who you are before it depends on size or price.

**Small and owner managed businesses.** DK CPA and Rose Snyder & Jacobs both build around closely held companies and the families behind them. Grobstein Teeple is the choice when the problem is a dispute rather than a return. GHJ is the strongest option for a nonprofit.

**Startups and funded companies.** Armanino is the only firm with a published price, which matters most at seed stage. SingerLewak carries the audit committee and IPO readiness experience for a later round. MGO handles public and private companies in the same practice.

**High net worth families and talent.** HCVT names family offices as a core client type and runs the largest Los Angeles based practice. NKSFB has the touring and recording infrastructure. Gursey Schneider is the one to call when a valuation or a divorce is involved.

## What do accounting firms in Los Angeles charge?

Eleven of the twelve firms publish no price at all.

Armanino is the exception. Its startup bundle costs $18,487 a year at the Launch tier and $26,887 a year at the Scale tier. Both require a one year contract.

The gap between those two tiers is a ceiling rather than a feature set. Launch covers up to 50 transactions and entities under $250,000 of net taxable income. Scale covers up to 100 transactions and entities under $10 million in assets.

Every other firm quotes on request. That is normal for professional services, and it means the only way to compare cost is to run the same scope past several firms.

## Which firms are actually in the City of Los Angeles?

All twelve firms ranked here publish a street address inside the City of Los Angeles. Four well known firms that rank for this search do not.

Miller Kaplan prints the heading "LOS ANGELES" above the address 3900 W. Alameda Avenue, Suite 2400, Burbank. That address is confirmed on its locations page, its contact page and its managing partner's own biography. The same page labels a Hailey, Idaho office "Sun Valley."

Prager Metis publishes three California offices, in El Segundo, Torrance and San Jose. None is in the City of Los Angeles. A firm release from 2015 celebrates 50 years in Los Angeles, but no Los Angeles address appears on the site today.

Velin & Associates owns the domain losangelescpa.org and works from West Hollywood. Clear Peak Accounting publishes a "Top CPA Firms in Los Angeles" list and ranks itself on it. The firm is based in Santa Monica.

A postal address is not the same thing as a city. Beverly Hills, Santa Monica, West Hollywood, Culver City, Burbank and Glendale are all separate cities with their own councils and their own business taxes.

## Which firms are owned by private equity?

Three of the twelve are not independent. Only two of them say so.

CohnReznick announced a "strategic growth investment" from the Apax Funds on 26 February 2025, in its own release. That release also split the firm into a licensed CPA entity and an unlicensed advisory entity.

Armanino appears on Further Global Capital Management's portfolio page with the year 2024. Armanino's own press release index stops in January 2023 and carries nothing about the investment.

NKSFB joined Focus Financial Partners in 2018. Its history page still describes Focus as "NASDAQ: FOCS." That is no longer accurate. The site says nothing about ownership since.

Ownership is not a verdict. It changes who the firm answers to, how fast it is expected to grow, and what happens to your partner relationship after a second transaction.

## What do the SEC and PCAOB records show?

Five of the twelve firms carry a finding from a federal regulator.

[The SEC censured HCVT and fined it $300,000 on 13 September 2018](https://www.sec.gov/files/litigation/admin/2018/34-84118.pdf). The order covered independence violations across 57 private fund audits and six broker dealer audits, and it barred the firm from certain audit work for one year.

[The SEC ordered CohnReznick to return $1.9 million to investors on 8 June 2022](https://www.sec.gov/newsroom/press-releases/2022-102) over the Longfin and Sequential audits. [The PCAOB then censured the firm and fined it $20,000 on 11 July 2023](https://pcaobus.org/news-events/news-releases/news-release-detail/pcaob-sanctions-cohnreznick-llp-for-failing-to-make-required-disclosures-on-a-timely-basis) for filing required disclosures about five months late.

[The PCAOB found deficiencies in four of four Armanino audits it reviewed](https://assets.pcaobus.org/pcaob-dev/docs/default-source/inspections/reports/documents/104-2025-001-armanino.pdf), in a report issued on 21 November 2024. [It found deficiencies in two of two Rose Snyder & Jacobs audits](https://assets.pcaobus.org/pcaob-dev/docs/default-source/inspections/reports/documents/104-2024-172-rose-snyder.pdf), in a report issued on 24 October 2024.

An inspection deficiency is not a fine. It means the inspectors judged the evidence in the file insufficient to support the opinion the firm signed.

The largest penalty in this research belongs to a firm that did not qualify. [The SEC settled with Prager Metis for $1.95 million on 17 September 2024](https://www.sec.gov/newsroom/press-releases/2024-133). The order covered the FTX audits and indemnification clauses in more than 200 engagement letters. The SEC found the firm falsely represented that those audits met Generally Accepted Auditing Standards. It also found the firm missed the risk arising from FTX's relationship with Alameda Research LLC. A search of pragermetis.com for FTX returns no results.

## How we ranked the firms

Every firm had to publish a street address inside the City of Los Angeles on its own website.

That single test removed more candidates than anything else. A suite in Burbank, Beverly Hills or Santa Monica did not count. Neither did a mailbox store.

Ownership had to be traceable. Where a firm is owned by a buyout fund, the entry names the fund and the year. It also says whether the firm discloses it.

Regulator records were pulled from the SEC and the PCAOB directly, not from news coverage. Where a firm has a finding, it appears inside that firm's entry rather than in a footnote.

Site currency counted. A copyright year eight years out of date, a broken staff directory or a placeholder statistic tells a buyer something about how the firm runs.

No estimates were used. Where a firm publishes no headcount, no founding year and no price, the entry says so instead of filling the gap.

Review scores are absent throughout. Accounting firms have no equivalent of the G2 and Capterra listings used elsewhere on this site. Google and Yelp both blocked every research pass, so no score was collected rather than an unverified one published.

Every firm here is a certified public accountant firm, or a partnership that keeps its audit work inside a licensed CPA entity. Armanino and CohnReznick are the two that split, and both say so in their own footers.

## When none of these firms is the right choice

Twelve firms is not every firm, and a Los Angeles address is not always the thing that matters most.

A company with no California operations gains little from a Los Angeles firm. Federal and multi-state work travels. The firm that already knows your state filings will usually cost less than one learning them.

A pre-revenue company is the wrong fit for everything on this list. Armanino's $18,487 a year is the cheapest published figure here, and every other firm quotes on request.

An owner who wants the same person on every call may do better with a sole practitioner. Every firm here that publishes a partner count reports more than six.

If the work is a single tax return, none of this matters. Address, ownership and inspection history are questions for a recurring engagement.

## How do I check a Los Angeles accounting firm myself?

Start with the address on the firm's own locations page, and read the city on the address line rather than the heading above it.

Then search the firm name at sec.gov and pcaobus.org. Both hold public orders and inspection reports, and neither appears on any marketing page.

The [California Board of Accountancy publishes licence status for every CPA and CPA firm in the state](https://www.dca.ca.gov/cba/consumers/lookup.shtml). Its lookup sits behind a human verification step, so it has to be run by hand.

Three questions are worth asking on the first call. Who owns the firm today, and has that changed in the last three years. Which legal entity signs the engagement letter, and is that entity a licensed CPA firm. What did the most recent peer review report say.

## Which firms were excluded, and why?

Eight firms that rank for this search did not qualify.

Miller Kaplan and Prager Metis were excluded on address. Miller Kaplan's "Los Angeles" office is in Burbank. Prager Metis publishes no City of Los Angeles address at all.

Kruze Consulting and Propeller Industries were excluded on the same test. Kruze publishes one office, in San Francisco. Its Los Angeles landing page returns a 404. Propeller publishes one address, at One World Trade Center in New York. It describes its own roles as fully remote.

Karlin & Peebles merged into the law firm Holland & Knight on 1 January 2025. Its contact page now prints Holland & Knight's address. Kirsch Kohn & Bridge no longer has a site of its own, and kkbcpa.com redirects to aprio.com.

Cooper Moss Resnick Klein has a qualifying Sherman Oaks address. Its newest datable content is a 2015 tax letter, and four spam subdomains are serving foreign language pages from the firm's own domain.

Fineman West could not be verified. The domain finemanwest.com would not serve. The live site publishes no address, no phone number and no footer.
